WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:07.150
Every three years we revalue your property
and update your rating valuation or RV.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:13.190
The RV is what we estimate your property will
sell for on 1 August this year.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:17.970
The RV isn’t a market value and shouldn’t
be used for insurance or property sales,

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.289
it’s purely for the purpose of rates.

00:00:20.289 --> 00:00:24.859
It’s important to know that an increase
in your property value doesn't necessarily

00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:27.130
mean you’ll pay more in rates.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:30.900
To explain that, we need to look at how rates
are set:

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.580
Rates are just one source of Council income
and their level depends on funding decisions

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.820
made by the Council.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:42.270
Property value only makes up a portion of
your rates; it doesn’t affect fixed charges

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:46.220
rates based on land-area or targeted rates
such as the

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:49.320
kerbside collection service you chose.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.720
Revaluation doesn’t increase the total amount
the Council collects from rates,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.960
instead it helps us work out everyone’s share.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.700
If your property has a lower than average
valuation increase, it’s likely to have

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.550
no effect on your rates and in some cases
may mean you pay less.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:11.690
While a higher than average valuation increase
could result in higher rates.

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:16.820
We won’t know how your new property value will impact your rates until the start of the next rates year.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.200
You can object to your rating valuation and
details on how to do this

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.780
will be on the notices sent to property owners.

